      "id": 2,
      "claim": "A breach of that duty requires a failure to meet the applicable standard of care; merely violating an internal hospital rule (oral order instead of written) is not per se a breach unless it causes injury.",
      "evidence": "Hospital policies are evidence of the standard of care but are not binding legal rules; a reasonable physician could give an oral order that is promptly documented without breaching the standard.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The doctor's conduct was not the proximate cause of the father's death because the mislabeling of the blood was an independent negligent act by a blood‑bank employee that broke the causal chain.",
      "evidence": "The person who received the nurse’s call correctly wrote down the order for A‑positive blood; the error occurred later when an unidentified blood‑bank worker mislabeled the unit, an act not foreseeable from the oral order.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The blood‑bank employee who supplied the wrong blood is not an agent or employee of the doctor, so the doctor is not vicariously liable for that employee’s negligence.",
      "evidence": "The nurse and blood‑bank staff are employed by the hospital; physicians are only liable for the negligence of staff they directly supervise, which is not shown here.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because there is no breach that was a proximate cause of the injury and no agency relationship creating vicarious liability, the mother’s wrongful‑death claim against the doctor will fail.",
      "evidence": "Negligence requires duty, breach, causation, and damages; the doctor’s conduct fails the breach‑and‑causation elements.",
